NAME
       path_join, resolve_path, cervus_confirm - shared helpers

SYNOPSIS
       #include <cervus_util.h>

       void path_join(const char *base, const char *name, char *out);
       void path_norm(char *path);
       void resolve_path(const char *cwd, const char *path, char *out);

       int  cervus_check_help_version(int argc, char **argv,
                                      const char *usage, const char *ver);
       int  cervus_confirm(const char *what, const char *target);
       const char *cervus_path_danger(const char *path);

       int  util_readline(int fd, char *buf, int maxlen);
       void util_write_stdout(const char *s);
       void util_write_stderr(const char *s);

DESCRIPTION
       Helpers shared by the command-line tools, defined inline in the
       header.

       path_join concatenates a directory and a name; path_norm folds
       away . and .. in place; resolve_path turns a relative path into
       an absolute one against a working directory.

       cervus_check_help_version handles --help and --version the same
       way in every tool.

       cervus_path_danger names the reason a path is dangerous to
       remove or overwrite, such as a root directory or a mount point,
       and cervus_confirm asks before going ahead. Destructive tools
       use the pair so the prompt is worded the same everywhere.

RETURN VALUE
       cervus_confirm returns non-zero when the user agreed;
       cervus_path_danger returns NULL when the path is ordinary.

SEE ALSO
       rm(1), wipefs(1), mkfs(1)
